      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine the prevalences and clustering of risk factors for coronary artery disease among unselected patients admitted to the first level of hospitalization."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The survey was prospectively designed to review all the medical records of patients discharged in the week starting at 0800 h on 21 October 1996. Family and personal histories of common risk factors, major routine biochemical data at entry, treatments during hospitalization and diagnoses at discharge (ICD-9 codes) were examined. In total 235 divisions of internal medicine uniformly distributed throughout Italy took part in the survey."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The survey shows that unselected patients admitted to divisions of internal medicine are characterized by a high prevalence of risk factors for coronary artery disease. Some of these are incompletely assessed. Definition of the clustering of risk factors is also far from optimal."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The risk profiles of unselected patients at cardiovascular risk hospitalized in divisions of internal medicine are incompletely assessed. Several reasons may prevent a thorough detection of risk factors and consequently their treatment. There is much potential for improvement."
        }
      ]},
      "ArticleTitle": "Assessment of risk factors for coronary artery disease in divisions of internal medicine. The FAPOI Study Group. Federazione delle Associazioni dei Primari Ospedalieri Internisti.",
